WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.356
Life without parole,
for murdering his wife.

00:00:03.576 --> 00:00:06.014
Two years later: not guilty.

00:00:06.464 --> 00:00:08.008
His name is Russ Faria.

00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:12.465
And today, prosecutors have charged
someone else with his wife's murder:

00:00:12.465 --> 00:00:14.970
the friend who drove her home that night.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:20.139
Days before the killing, that friend had
been made the sole beneficiary of the

00:00:20.139 --> 00:00:22.783
victim's $150,000 life insurance policy.

00:00:25.723 --> 00:00:28.468
So how did a man with
four alibi witnesses,

00:00:28.468 --> 00:00:31.941
a receipt and a phone trail
end up with a life sentence?

00:00:32.281 --> 00:00:36.655
And why did it take nearly ten years to
charge the last person known to have seen

00:00:36.655 --> 00:00:37.655
her alive?

00:00:40.459 --> 00:00:46.674
December 27th, 2011. Troy, Missouri, a
small town about an hour northwest of St.

00:00:46.674 --> 00:00:47.674
Louis.

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:51.782
Betsy Faria was 42,
a mother of two daughters.

00:00:52.122 --> 00:00:55.825
She was also fighting cancer that
had spread to her liver. That day,

00:00:55.825 --> 00:00:57.276
she'd had chemotherapy.

00:00:57.616 --> 00:01:00.563
That evening, her friend
Pam Hupp drove her home.

00:01:00.903 --> 00:01:04.979
According to later reporting, Hupp
had insisted on giving her the ride.

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:08.704
Betsy's husband, Russ,
was somewhere else entirely:

00:01:08.704 --> 00:01:13.633
at his regular Tuesday game night with
four friends, more than 20 miles away.

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:17.766
On his way home, he stopped at
an Arby's. There was a receipt,

00:01:17.766 --> 00:01:19.383
and surveillance video.

00:01:19.723 --> 00:01:23.328
At 9:40 that night, Russ called 911.

00:01:23.828 --> 00:01:26.440
He had found Betsy dead inside their home.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.485
She had been stabbed 55 times.

00:01:30.285 --> 00:01:35.467
Days later, after Betsy's funeral, Russ
was arrested and charged with first-degree

00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:36.467
murder.

00:01:38.773 --> 00:01:41.606
Prosecutors built their
case around the husband.

00:01:41.946 --> 00:01:46.358
Pam Hupp testified at that trial,
as a witness for the prosecution.

00:01:46.698 --> 00:01:50.831
They pointed to a pair of slippers
they said were stained with blood.

00:01:51.171 --> 00:01:54.304
They told jurors his friends
were covering for him,

00:01:54.304 --> 00:01:56.888
and that his frantic 911 call was an act.

00:01:57.228 --> 00:02:01.059
But the jury never heard the
defense's case about someone else.

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:07.321
Days before she died, Betsy had changed
the beneficiary of her $150,000 life

00:02:07.321 --> 00:02:10.497
insurance policy, from Russ to Pam Hupp.

00:02:10.837 --> 00:02:14.368
Hupp said Betsy wanted the money
to go to her daughters one day.

00:02:14.708 --> 00:02:19.172
And according to the Post-Dispatch, Hupp's
own account of that evening kept changing.

00:02:19.512 --> 00:02:23.674
She first told investigators she
hadn't gone inside the house. Later,

00:02:23.674 --> 00:02:27.060
that she'd gone as far as
the living room. Later still,

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.113
that she'd been in the bedroom.

00:02:29.453 --> 00:02:34.150
Phone records showed her making a call
at 7:04 p.m. from near the Faria home,

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:37.545
and those records would
become a key point of dispute.

00:02:37.885 --> 00:02:42.454
But at Russ's first trial, the judge did
not allow the defense to argue that Hupp

00:02:42.454 --> 00:02:43.820
could be the killer.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.924
Missouri law required evidence directly
connecting another suspect to the crime,

00:02:48.924 --> 00:02:52.082
and the judge ruled that
standard had not been met.

00:02:52.422 --> 00:02:57.776
On November 21st, 2013, the
jury found Russ Faria guilty.

00:02:58.116 --> 00:03:02.632
The sentence: life in prison
without parole, plus 30 years.

00:03:05.732 --> 00:03:07.961
The insurance payout went to Pam Hupp.

00:03:08.301 --> 00:03:10.983
Betsy's daughters sued for it. They lost.

00:03:11.483 --> 00:03:13.831
An appeals court said the form named Hupp,

00:03:13.831 --> 00:03:18.135
and that Betsy's intentions beyond that
could not be determined from the evidence.

00:03:20.675 --> 00:03:23.529
Russ's lawyer, Joel
Schwartz, kept fighting.

00:03:23.869 --> 00:03:28.905
In 2015, the case went back to
the trial court, and on June 5th,

00:03:28.905 --> 00:03:35.092
Judge Steven Ohmer, a St. Louis judge
assigned to the case, ordered a new trial.

00:03:35.432 --> 00:03:38.056
His ruling centered on the
evidence about Pam Hupp.

00:03:38.396 --> 00:03:41.563
Days later, Russ walked
out of jail on bond,

00:03:41.563 --> 00:03:44.921
after about three and a
half years behind bars.

00:03:45.261 --> 00:03:51.059
In November 2015, he was tried again. This
time, he chose to have a judge decide,

00:03:51.059 --> 00:03:52.192
not a jury.

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:55.504
And this time, the defense
could point to Pam Hupp.

00:03:55.844 --> 00:04:01.108
On November 6th, 2015, Judge
Ohmer found Russ Faria not guilty.

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:05.754
And the judge openly criticized the
investigation that had put him in prison.

00:04:08.394 --> 00:04:14.743
Nine months later, in August 2016, Pam
Hupp called 911 from her home in O'Fallon,

00:04:14.743 --> 00:04:15.838
Missouri.

00:04:16.178 --> 00:04:20.209
She had shot and killed a man:
Louis Gumpenberger, 33,

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:22.493
who lived with a disability.

00:04:22.833 --> 00:04:26.781
She told police he was an intruder,
and that he'd demanded "Russ's money."

00:04:27.121 --> 00:04:31.022
In his pocket, police found
$900 in cash and a note:

00:04:31.022 --> 00:04:35.462
apparent instructions to kidnap
Hupp and get "Russ's money."

00:04:35.802 --> 00:04:38.841
Prosecutors said the cash and
the note had been planted,

00:04:38.841 --> 00:04:42.860
and that the serial numbers ran in
sequence with bills found in Hupp's home.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.614
They said she had lured him by posing
as a producer for NBC's Dateline,

00:04:48.614 --> 00:04:52.060
offering $1,000 to help
reenact a 911 call.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.765
Days earlier, another woman had
reported the same kind of approach.

00:04:57.105 --> 00:05:02.223
Prosecutors alleged it was an attempt
to frame Russ Faria all over again.

00:05:02.823 --> 00:05:07.948
In 2019, Hupp entered an Alford plea to
first-degree murder: not admitting guilt,

00:05:07.948 --> 00:05:11.777
but conceding the state had
enough evidence to convict her.

00:05:12.117 --> 00:05:14.648
She was sentenced to life without parole.

00:05:14.988 --> 00:05:19.420
Investigators also took another
look at a death from 2013.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.304
Hupp's mother, Shirley Neumann, had died
after falling from her balcony at a senior

00:05:24.304 --> 00:05:25.427
living facility.

00:05:25.767 --> 00:05:28.878
Her death was ruled an accident. In 2017,

00:05:28.878 --> 00:05:33.710
the medical examiner changed the
manner of death to "undetermined."

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.151
No one has been charged in her death.

00:05:39.151 --> 00:05:43.839
In 2020, Russ Faria settled his
civil rights lawsuit for $2 million,

00:05:43.839 --> 00:05:46.247
with no admission of wrongdoing.

00:05:46.587 --> 00:05:52.040
Then, in July 2021, Lincoln County
Prosecutor Mike Wood charged Pam Hupp with

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:55.259
first-degree murder in
Betsy Faria's death.

00:05:55.599 --> 00:05:57.113
She pleaded not guilty.

00:05:57.453 --> 00:06:01.780
Wood also opened an investigation into
how the original case was handled.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:06.370
In 2025, a former sheriff's
captain on the case, Mike Merkel,

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:10.684
was charged with perjury over
his testimony in the Faria case.

00:06:10.684 --> 00:06:14.236
He is presumed innocent,
and that case is pending.

00:06:14.576 --> 00:06:19.039
The original prosecutor, Leah
Askey, has denied any wrongdoing.

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:22.802
Hupp's trial has been moved
and delayed again and again.

00:06:23.142 --> 00:06:27.524
Her lawyers have pointed to DNA from
an unidentified man on evidence,

00:06:27.524 --> 00:06:31.790
including the knife handle.
Prosecutors dispute that it clears her.

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.311
In April 2026, Hupp waived
her right to a jury,

00:06:35.311 --> 00:06:38.972
and the state dropped its
pursuit of the death penalty.

00:06:39.312 --> 00:06:44.229
A judge is scheduled to hear
the case in January 2028.

00:06:46.929 --> 00:06:51.589
Russ Faria spent about three and a half
years locked up for a murder he was

00:06:51.589 --> 00:06:53.315
ultimately acquitted of.

00:06:53.655 --> 00:06:58.539
Pam Hupp is serving life for killing
Louis Gumpenberger. In Betsy's case,

00:06:58.539 --> 00:07:02.186
she is charged, not convicted,
and presumed innocent.

00:07:02.526 --> 00:07:05.230
Betsy Faria was 42 years old.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:10.834
Nearly fifteen years after her death,
no court has decided who killed her.
